  "body": "https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/form-control-infrastructure.html\r\n\r\nThe spec above says when getting selectionStart/End, if cannot be applied to current element, it returns `null`. Meanwhile, it also says when setting any value to selectionStart/End and selectionStart/End cannot be applied to the current element, throw exception.\r\n\r\nThis make below code snippet throw exception:\r\n```javascript\r\n// input is input[type=radio]\r\nlet node = input\r\nfor(let key in node) {\r\n  node[key] = node[key]\r\n}\r\n```\r\n\r\nSo it tells us the value of selectionStart is `null`, but selectionStart cannot be set with a `null` value? It feels inconsistent for allowed values for these properties, and it also make copying properties between different inputs especially difficult.\r\n\r\nIf it does not support selection at all, why not simply remove the property from the element completely, rather than leaving it return `null`?\r\n\r\nIf there is really a reason to leave this property on elements such as `input[type=radio]`, then I think it is reasonable to not throw an exception when setting `input.selectionStart = null`. \r\n\r\nIf somebody was setting the value to `null`, apparently they are not expecting it to make any selection, then why bother throw an exception?\r\n",
